    "title": "No. 16 Siena nearly upsets No. 1 Duke in NCAA tournament while making only 1 sub ... for 2 seconds",
    "text": "Siena University came so close to March Madness history — in more ways than one. The 16th-seeded Saints came within a few points of a massive upset over Duke, and they did it with all five of Siena’s starters playing almost the full 40 minutes.Though the Blue Devils entered the game as 28-point favorites, Siena quickly took control of the game, leading Duke by double-digits at halftime. Duke ended up surging late for the 71-65 win, salvaging their March Madness run as the No. 1 overall seed.AdvertisementSiena made a single substitution in the final seconds: With 10 seconds remaining, Brendan Coyle was taken out in favor of Christian Jones, the sole bench player to make an appearance in the game. Coyle, who had four fouls, was subbed back in two seconds later after a Siena foul.Yahoo Fantasy Bracket Mayhem: Make your picks for $50K in total prizesThat sub kept the team from playing all the way through with the same starting five, though Coyle is still credited as playing 40 minutes. Still, it was a valiant effort from Siena, who nearly pulled it off despite zero rest.Those five players were grinding for the Saints throughout the game, going a combined 25-of-65 from the field and nabbing 31 rebounds. Sophomore guard Gavin Doty led the team with 21 points, followed by freshman forward Francis Folefac with 18 points and seven rebounds, and Coyle, a senior forward, with 12 points. Senior guard Justice Shoats and graduate center Riley Mulvey both got on the board as well.AdvertisementThree players — Coyle, Folefac and Mulvey — ended with four fouls as a result of their hard play. (Head coach Gerry McNamara may have been lucky that none of them fouled out.)The last school to play an NCAA tournament game without taking out any starters was DePaul in 1979, during a national semifinal.The no-subs strategy may have come with positives and negatives. The grind and the consistency were certainly there. But, foul trouble aside, the Saints starters simply looked gassed by the final minutes, and lost significant steam in the second half.Postgame, McNamara justified the choice, adding that he was particularly proud of his team for (mostly) shutting down Duke star Cameron Boozer.Advertisement“The way we looked, the way it felt to me, I went with a group that I thought could give us the best chance,” McNamara said.Duke also relied heavily on its starters, though not quite as extremely as Siena. Only two bench players got playing time on Thursday, with Nikolas Khamenia’s second-half 3-pointer marking the only bench points for the team. Meanwhile, the Boozer brothers, Cameron and Cayden, played 39 minutes each in the win, combining for 41 points.Postgame, though, Duke head coach Jon Scheyer admitted that McNamara, who Scheyer says he’s known “for a long time,” was the better coach on Thursday.Advertisement“G-Mac had his guys way more ready to play than I did. He out-coached me, he out-coached us,” Scheyer said. “That’s one of the hardest moments for me in sport, period … They played at such a high level.”\"[Gerry McNamara] had his guys way more ready to play than we did. He out coached me.”Duke HC Jon Scheyer after narrowly escaping 16-seed Siena 😮‍💨 pic.twitter.com/VaIO16Ujgi— Yahoo Sports (@YahooSports) March 19, 2026The Blue Devils will now move on to play No. 9 TCU in the second round, after the Horned Frogs beat Ohio State in the final seconds earlier on Thursday.",
